WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:04.074
<v Maya>You know, usually when a new machine comes along, there's this expectation of like total precision.</v>

2
00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:05.115
<v Daniel>Right. Like engineering.</v>

3
00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:15.023
<v Maya>Exactly. A machine rolls in. It does the exact physical or mental job a human used to do. And the economist just points at a chart and says, there it is. That job is gone.</v>

4
00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:23.229
<v Daniel>It's a really comforting narrative, honestly. We like economic shifts to be binary. We want to see the old factory close and the new one open.</v>

5
00:00:23.918 --> 00:00:32.081
<v Maya>Right. But then you step into the actual modern world of generative AI and freelance work, and suddenly that clean chart is just, well, completely broken. Yeah,</v>

6
00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:32.401
<v Daniel>shattered.</v>

7
00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:43.485
<v Maya>If you look at the freelance labor market today, it's not a story of one-to-one replacement at all. It is murky, it's complicated, and frankly, for a lot of people trying to make a living, it's pretty chaotic.</v>

8
00:00:44.045 --> 00:00:51.128
<v Daniel>Which is exactly why we need to look beyond the simplistic headlines. We have to actually examine the plumbing of what's happening to human labor right now.</v>

9
00:00:51.543 --> 00:01:00.072
<v Maya>And we have the perfect lens for you today. Welcome to this custom-tailored deep dive where we are exploring a brand new report that literally just posted to Archive this week.</v>

10
00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:02.295
<v Daniel>Yeah, just on Sunday, November 15, 2026.</v>

11
00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:09.022
<v Maya>Exactly. It's called The Rebundling of Freelance Work in the AI Age. And this is part of the Work at the Frontier series.</v>

12
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:10.364
<v Daniel>It's quite a collaboration, too.</v>

13
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:22.296
<v Maya>It is. It's co-authored by researchers from OpenAI, Caroline Chin, Alex Martin-Richmond, and Carl Shan, alongside researchers from Upwork, which are Shagun Kala, Nadav Kunievsky, and Tang Liu. I</v>

14
00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:24.296
<v Daniel>mean, it's a massive data set.</v>

15
00:01:24.596 --> 00:01:30.098
<v Maya>They are bringing together the company building the AI and the company hosting the freelance labor.</v>

16
00:01:30.638 --> 00:01:31.678
<v Daniel>Which is so rare to see.</v>

17
00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:40.280
<v Maya>And they are trying to answer one core question for us. When people can do work directly in chat GPT, what happens to the work they still pay humans for?</v>

18
00:01:40.913 --> 00:01:42.200
<v Daniel>That's the million dollar question.</v>

19
00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:57.444
<v Maya>Literally. And to really ground this, we've also pulled in four foundational background papers that set the theoretical stage for this new data. Okay, let's unpack this. And I see your backdrop has already shifted to a bustling futuristic digital cityscape overlaid with</v>

20
00:01:57.504 --> 00:01:58.405
<v Maya>dynamic data charts.</v>

21
00:01:58.745 --> 00:02:11.493
<v Daniel>Yeah, I thought it was fitting. That digital cityscape represents the evolving labor market we're looking at today. You can see old structures being torn down, but the raw materials are being rapidly reassembled into entirely new skyscrapers.</v>

22
00:02:11.902 --> 00:02:17.483
<v Maya>I love that visual. Let's start with tearing down that first big assumption, though. Simple substitution.</v>

23
00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:21.244
<v Daniel>Right. The idea that one AI request equals one fired freelancer.</v>

24
00:02:21.364 --> 00:02:27.886
<v Maya>Exactly. The new paper starts by looking at what people actually do with AI versus what they hire humans for on Upwork.</v>

25
00:02:28.086 --> 00:02:37.308
<v Daniel>And it immediately throws cold water on that one to one replacement idea. If you look at the Exhibit 2.1 in the report, the divergence between AI use and human hiring is striking.</v>

26
00:02:37.608 --> 00:02:38.768
<v Maya>What are the actual numbers there?</v>

27
00:02:39.283 --> 00:02:51.246
<v Daniel>Well, they compared a sample of chat GPT conversations to Upwork job postings to see the activity mix. Content writing makes up 14.45% of the sampled chat GPT conversations.</v>

28
00:02:51.326 --> 00:02:52.887
<v Maya>So a huge chunk of what people are doing.</v>

29
00:02:52.967 --> 00:03:01.209
<v Daniel>Exactly. People are clearly using it heavily to draft text. But content writing makes up only about 1.27% of Upwork posts.</v>

30
00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:04.870
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So people are just doing the writing themselves with the AI.</v>

31
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:05.750
<v Daniel>Yeah, pretty much.</v>

32
00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:15.518
<v Maya>It sounds like ChatGPT is becoming the new DIY tool for advice and drafting, while clients are hiring freelancers to be the general contractors who build the final house.</v>

33
00:03:15.758 --> 00:03:22.864
<v Daniel>That's a great analogy. Because when you look at the Upwork side of this data, the demand leans heavily toward technical implementation and visual production.</v>

34
00:03:23.004 --> 00:03:23.825
<v Maya>Right, like the hard stuff.</v>

35
00:03:24.085 --> 00:03:38.955
<v Daniel>Exactly. The report tracks shifts from the first half of 2025 to the first half of 2026. Mobile development gained 2.27 percentage points in share. Video and animation gained 1.38 points. But meanwhile,</v>

36
00:03:39.355 --> 00:03:42.557
<v Daniel>standard web development actually lost 1.98 points.</v>

37
00:03:42.917 --> 00:03:49.982
<v Maya>Wait, we really need to pause on why that is happening. Why would mobile development grow while standard web development shrinks?</v>

38
00:03:50.337 --> 00:04:02.429
<v Daniel>It comes down to friction and complexity. Standard web development has become heavily commoditized. You have template-driven sites, and AI is incredibly good at generating standard HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</v>

39
00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:06.392
<v Maya>So a client can easily spin that up themselves or just with minimal help.</v>

40
00:04:06.513 --> 00:04:09.796
<v Daniel>Exactly. But mobile is different, highly different.</v>

41
00:04:09.996 --> 00:04:10.917
<v Maya>Because of the app stores.</v>

42
00:04:11.242 --> 00:04:24.253
<v Daniel>Yeah, that's a big part of it. Mobile development requires native integration with phone hardware, testing across dozens of device types, navigating the convoluted policies of Apple and Google app stores, and optimizing battery drain.</v>

43
00:04:24.693 --> 00:04:26.575
<v Maya>Stuff the AI just can't do on its own.</v>

44
00:04:26.735 --> 00:04:36.283
<v Daniel>Right. AI can write a snippet of Swift code, but it cannot navigate the final mile of delivering a polished, store-ready app. Same with video and animation.</v>

45
00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:38.645
<v Maya>Like AI can make a static image, sure.</v>

46
00:04:39.041 --> 00:04:50.373
<v Daniel>Or a short video clip, yeah. But assembling a cohesive, emotionally resonant, three-minute animated explainer video? That requires a human to manage the artistic vision and the whole computing pipeline.</v>

47
00:04:50.743 --> 00:04:59.647
<v Maya>Okay, so the activities that require specialized integration or the delivery of a complex finished visual or technical product, those are the ones growing.</v>

48
00:04:59.887 --> 00:05:09.591
<v Daniel>Exactly. And to try and capture this shift, the authors formalize this divergence in Exhibit 2.2. They introduce a metric they call chat GPT intensity.</v>

49
00:05:10.392 --> 00:05:11.232
<v Maya>Okay, lay it on me.</v>

50
00:05:11.662 --> 00:05:21.779
<v Daniel>Well, the ChatGPT intensity measure is defined as the log ratio of a category's ChatGPT share to its baseline Upwork share back in early 2023.</v>

51
00:05:23.109 --> 00:05:33.731
<v Maya>Hold on. Log ratio. Let's translate that for someone who is just, you know, walking their dog right now and hasn't looked at a math textbook in a decade. In plain English, what is this actually measuring?</v>

52
00:05:33.952 --> 00:05:43.954
<v Daniel>Fair enough. Fair enough. Think of it as a scale of how heavily an activity leans toward direct AI use compared to how often clients historically hired remote human labor for it.</v>

53
00:05:44.294 --> 00:05:44.594
<v Maya>Got it.</v>

54
00:05:45.194 --> 00:05:54.417
<v Daniel>So if an activity has high chat GPT intensity, it means people are overwhelmingly just talking to the chat bot to get it done. rather than hiring a human.</v>

55
00:05:55.237 --> 00:06:06.902
<v Maya>OK, so logically, if a specific task has high chat GPT intensity, you'd expect the freelance demand for that exact task on Upwork to plummet, right? Like, it's DIY now.</v>

56
00:06:07.062 --> 00:06:12.144
<v Daniel>Right. And for 43 out of the 63 subcategories they matched, that logic holds up perfectly.</v>

57
00:06:12.345 --> 00:06:12.605
<v Maya>OK.</v>

58
00:06:12.745 --> 00:06:25.791
<v Daniel>High AI intensity meant losing marketplace share, and low AI intensity meant gaining it. It fits a very clean substitution or complement pattern. But what's fascinating here is the other 20 subcategories.</v>

59
00:06:26.412 --> 00:06:34.958
<v Maya>Right, because exactly 20 of them do not fit that pattern at all. Almost a third of the categories completely defy this simple logic.</v>

60
00:06:35.318 --> 00:06:47.747
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's wild. You have areas with low chat GPT intensity that are still somehow losing freelance share and areas with high chat GPT intensity that are actually gaining freelance share.</v>

61
00:06:48.048 --> 00:06:50.289
<v Maya>Which makes no sense if it's just a one-to-one replacement.</v>

62
00:06:50.409 --> 00:07:04.294
<v Daniel>Exactly. Those 20 outliers are the proof that simple substitution explains only part of the picture. Client needs are fundamentally changing. If it were just about replacing humans, the math would be perfectly linear. But it's not.</v>

63
00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:17.203
<v Maya>But this brings up a huge tension, though, because if sub-imitution isn't a one-to-one thing, and it's not just a simple story of clients firing humans, we have to ask, how are the workers actually faring in this shifting landscape?</v>

64
00:07:17.723 --> 00:07:20.045
<v Daniel>That is the crucial flip side of this.</v>

65
00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:31.937
<v Maya>Right, because this new 2026 paper is heavily focused on the demand side, what clients are putting money down for. But if we look at our background sources, the reality for the workers themselves has been pretty brutal.</v>

66
00:07:32.377 --> 00:07:41.479
<v Daniel>It has been an incredibly difficult transition. We really have to bring in the 2024 paper by Hui, Reshef, and Zhou, published in Organization Science here.</v>

67
00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.420
<v Maya>Yes, that one was eye-opening.</v>

68
00:07:44.015 --> 00:07:56.108
<v Daniel>They used a rigorous causal difference in differences design to look at the immediate aftermath of ChatGPT's launch. And they found that freelancers in AI-exposed occupations lost employment and earnings across the board.</v>

69
00:07:56.489 --> 00:07:57.830
<v Maya>Even the really good ones, right?</v>

70
00:07:58.031 --> 00:08:01.074
<v Daniel>Yeah, even the top-tier, highly-skilled freelancers took a hit.</v>

71
00:08:01.417 --> 00:08:15.061
<v Maya>Let me stop you again for a second. Difference in differences in plain English. They just compared two groups of freelancers, those exposed to AI and those who weren't before and after chat GPT launch to isolate the real impact of the AI, right?</v>

72
00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:25.584
<v Daniel>Exactly. It's a statistical way to prove the AI caused the drop rather than, say, some broader economic recession. And the drop was very real.</v>

73
00:08:26.175 --> 00:08:38.108
<v Maya>And that panel lines perfectly with another background paper by Yu, Siemens, Raj, and Liu from early 2026. And it's worth noting Ten Liu is a co-author on both that paper and the new one we're discussing today.</v>

74
00:08:38.749 --> 00:08:41.372
<v Daniel>Right, providing a really cool bridge between the two studies.</v>

75
00:08:41.829 --> 00:08:56.618
<v Maya>Yeah. The You paper shows that after ChatGPT, freelancers started bidding on fewer jobs. Like, they were scrambling. They were repositioning horizontally into different skill categories or literally moving down to bid on lower</v>

76
00:08:56.738 --> 00:08:59.040
<v Maya>value jobs just to secure some kind of work.</v>

77
00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:10.507
<v Daniel>I mean, imagine what that feels like. You wake up, the types of gigs you've built your whole career on are suddenly vanishing or paying half as much, and you're desperately trying to rebrand your profile just to pay rent.</v>

78
00:09:11.913 --> 00:09:17.776
<v Maya>So I'm looking at these two sets of research and they seem to completely contradict each other. I'm genuinely confused here.</v>

79
00:09:18.236 --> 00:09:18.537
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

80
00:09:18.837 --> 00:09:33.525
<v Maya>Well, one set of papers says workers are losing money, panicking and bidding on cheaper jobs. But this new November 2026 report is telling a story about clients buying more complex work and demand evolving. Which is it? Does this new paper confirm that grim worker side</v>

81
00:09:33.545 --> 00:09:36.206
<v Maya>reality, soften it or tell a completely different story?</v>

82
00:09:36.737 --> 00:09:47.980
<v Daniel>It's not a contradiction so much as it is two sides of a very turbulent coin. The older papers show a worker-side struggle. The workers are suffering because they are trying to fund the old types of jobs.</v>

83
00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:48.640
<v Maya>Ah, okay.</v>

84
00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:59.343
<v Daniel>But the new OpenAI and Upwork paper is explicitly descriptive. They actually state they cannot identify direct causal substitution. Instead, they are tracking the demand side.</v>

85
00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.542
<v Maya>What the clients actually want now.</v>

86
00:10:01.762 --> 00:10:16.178
<v Daniel>Right. The tension resolves when you realize the clients aren't just buying less of the old work. They are actively funding bigger, newly recombined projects. The workers who haven't figured out how to deliver those new, larger projects are the ones scrambling.</v>

87
00:10:16.794 --> 00:10:31.429
<v Maya>Okay. Okay. So if the clients are shifting toward larger projects, we need to follow the money. We need to look exactly at how much clients are paying and what they're paying for, what they're buying. So let's look at Exhibit 2.4 in the new paper.</v>

88
00:10:32.069 --> 00:10:45.120
<v Daniel>The price and quantity margins here are where the story really takes a turn. When you look at AI-related fixed price budgets on Upwork, they are about 52% higher than non-AI-related projects. 52, that's huge.</v>

89
00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:45.761
<v Maya>That's huge.</v>

90
00:10:46.101 --> 00:10:52.207
<v Daniel>And that's even after controlling for the subcategory of the work and the month it was posted.</v>

91
00:10:53.622 --> 00:11:08.445
<v Maya>52% higher for a fixed price project. That is a massive premium. And on the surface, you'd think, great, freelancers are getting rich. They're making great money. But then you look at the hourly contracts. So the</v>

92
00:11:08.485 --> 00:11:15.287
<v Maya>posted hourly rates for AI-related jobs, they're only about 1.5% higher after controls.</v>

93
00:11:15.467 --> 00:11:17.007
<v Daniel>Right, which is...</v>

94
00:11:17.067 --> 00:11:20.007
<v Maya>So the price of a human hour is practically flat.</v>

95
00:11:20.228 --> 00:11:30.001
<v Daniel>But then look at the quantity of time purchased. Right. The hours worked in the first 90 days of those hourly contracts are about 41% higher for AI-related work.</v>

96
00:11:30.521 --> 00:11:36.123
<v Maya>Here's where it gets really interesting. Clients aren't paying a massive premium for a freelancer's hour.</v>

97
00:11:36.524 --> 00:11:40.145
<v Daniel>Right, they aren't saying, you use AI so your time is suddenly worth double.</v>

98
00:11:40.376 --> 00:11:53.124
<v Maya>Exactly. What they are doing is buying significantly more of those hours to fuel bigger, more ambitious projects. That 52% jump in fixed price budgets, that's not a wage premium. That is a scope premium.</v>

99
00:11:53.504 --> 00:12:01.389
<v Daniel>That is the crucial insight. And the authors explain this phenomenon through an incredible economic concept, the Jevons Paradox or the Jevons Rebound.</v>

100
00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:02.471
<v Maya>Break that down for us.</v>

101
00:12:02.891 --> 00:12:16.126
<v Daniel>So back in 1865, the economist William Stanley Jevons noticed something really counterintuitive about coal. When engineers made coal engines more efficient, you would assume people would use less coal to get the same amount of work done, right?</v>

102
00:12:16.507 --> 00:12:18.209
<v Maya>Yeah, that's what makes logical sense.</v>

103
00:12:18.552 --> 00:12:31.695
<v Daniel>but the exact opposite happened. The efficiency made coal power so economically viable that people started using it for vastly more applications. Coal consumption skyrocketed because ambition skyrocketed. Oh, wow.</v>

104
00:12:32.056 --> 00:12:44.959
<v Maya>So applying that to Upwork in 2026, by making individual tasks like writing code or drafting copy easier and cheaper with AI, clients aren't just saying, great, I saved 50 bucks on this website. I'll put it in my pocket.</v>

105
00:12:45.332 --> 00:12:50.855
<v Daniel>Nope. They're saying, wait, if the basic code takes half the time, my budget goes much further.</v>

106
00:12:51.215 --> 00:12:56.157
<v Maya>Let's build a bigger application. Let's add user analytics. Let's add custom motion graphics.</v>

107
00:12:56.517 --> 00:13:09.483
<v Daniel>Exactly. They are undertaking larger, far more labor-intensive projects than they ever would have attempted before. AI isn't just reducing the labor needed for a static project. It's expanding the boundaries of the projects themselves.</v>

108
00:13:10.094 --> 00:13:15.596
<v Maya>It's drawing in more human hours to achieve a higher quality, more complex final output.</v>

109
00:13:15.917 --> 00:13:16.377
<v Daniel>Precisely.</v>

110
00:13:16.957 --> 00:13:26.321
<v Maya>But if clients are buying 41% more hours for these bigger projects, what exactly is inside them? What does a supersized AI-era project actually look like?</v>

111
00:13:27.142 --> 00:13:29.343
<v Daniel>This brings us to the core concept of the paper.</v>

112
00:13:29.803 --> 00:13:32.204
<v Maya>Which is right there in the title, rebundling.</v>

113
00:13:32.673 --> 00:13:47.663
<v Daniel>Let's look at how the DNA of a job posting is mutating. The report maps that tasks clients are requesting into specific clusters. In early 2023, the effective number of task clusters in AI-related posts was 14.6. By late</v>

114
00:13:47.683 --> 00:13:48.224
<v Daniel>2025, that rose to 21.2.</v>

115
00:13:48.244 --> 00:13:48.564
<v Maya>That is about a</v>

116
00:13:49.224 --> 00:14:03.450
<v Daniel>45% jump in task diversity within a single category. And within individual job posts, the average number of distinct tasks requested is also slightly</v>

117
00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:08.772
<v Daniel>higher. 3.35 tasks in AI jobs versus 3.19 in non-AI jobs.</v>

118
00:14:08.992 --> 00:14:10.233
<v Maya>Okay, slightly more tasks.</v>

119
00:14:10.653 --> 00:14:23.939
<v Daniel>But the really wild statistic is about how those tasks are being combined. They isolated historically uncommon task pairs, meaning two things clients rarely ever asked for in the same job before the generative AI era.</v>

120
00:14:24.239 --> 00:14:24.819
<v Maya>Give me the numbers.</v>

121
00:14:25.657 --> 00:14:33.060
<v Daniel>Those uncommon pairs make up 8.4% of pairs in AI-related jobs. In other jobs, it's just 2.4%.</v>

122
00:14:33.581 --> 00:14:36.182
<v Maya>So they are mashing together weird combinations of skills.</v>

123
00:14:36.396 --> 00:14:49.327
<v Daniel>Yeah. But if you look at Appendix A of the report, it clarifies something really important about these mashups. 98.6% of these uncommon pairs join two tasks that already existed.</v>

124
00:14:49.628 --> 00:14:56.033
<v Maya>Oh, interesting. Yeah. Okay. So they aren't inventing entirely new sci-fi tasks out of</v>

125
00:14:56.053 --> 00:15:10.804
<v Daniel>nowhere. The novelty is purely in the combination. And mixed AI plus traditional pairs dominate. They note that this novelty is strongest in generative AI and creative production, where they see a roughly 60% lower historical prevalence for these exact pairings.</v>

126
00:15:10.964 --> 00:15:12.545
<v Maya>That's a massive shift.</v>

127
00:15:12.805 --> 00:15:27.254
<v Daniel>It is. But to understand the mechanism behind this, why these bundles are changing so dramatically, we have to look at another one of our background papers. Which one? The 2026 paper by Demmer, Horton, Imorlica, Lucier, and Shahidi called Chaining Tasks,</v>

128
00:15:27.714 --> 00:15:28.475
<v Daniel>Redefining Work.</v>

129
00:15:28.675 --> 00:15:32.988
<v Maya>Okay, so how does their theory explain these weird Upwork bundles?</v>

130
00:15:33.387 --> 00:15:41.533
<v Daniel>Well, Demerer and their co-authors explain that AI doesn't just perform isolated abstract actions. It executes contiguous chains of steps.</v>

131
00:15:41.733 --> 00:15:42.854
<v Maya>Contiguous chains.</v>

132
00:15:43.194 --> 00:15:58.145
<v Daniel>Right. Because of that, adjacency matters. If a human has a natural advantage on a specific step, say contextual judgment or physical integration or client communication, they can hand the adjacent tedious steps off to the AI as part of a larger</v>

133
00:15:58.205 --> 00:16:02.068
<v Daniel>chain and then take the AI's output to complete the final mile.</v>

134
00:16:02.437 --> 00:16:03.437
<v Maya>Oh, that makes total sense.</v>

135
00:16:03.658 --> 00:16:10.620
<v Daniel>Yeah. This perfectly explains why we are seeing these mixed AI plus traditional task pairs dominating the Upwork data.</v>

136
00:16:11.001 --> 00:16:19.824
<v Maya>Let's make this tangible. It sounds a lot like a modern chef, say, the freelancer overseeing a highly efficient sous chef, which would be the AI.</v>

137
00:16:20.204 --> 00:16:21.365
<v Daniel>That's a great way to put it.</v>

138
00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:36.279
<v Maya>The chef isn't chopping onions anymore. The AI handles that contiguous chain of prep work. But the chef doesn't just serve a bowl of raw chopped onions. They are combining those onions with complex sauces, existing tasks, in entirely novel ways to create a</v>

139
00:16:36.359 --> 00:16:42.083
<v Maya>broader menu. The re-bundled project isn't just a prepped ingredient. It's a massive multi-course meal.</v>

140
00:16:42.523 --> 00:16:52.784
<v Daniel>That analogy captures the dynamic perfectly. The human's economic value shifts away from executing the basic components, the chopped onions, and moves toward managing the interfaces.</v>

141
00:16:53.604 --> 00:16:59.167
<v Maya>They integrate the AI's output with traditional human skills to deliver that broader, more ambitious menu.</v>

142
00:16:59.367 --> 00:16:59.727
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

143
00:17:00.568 --> 00:17:13.474
<v Maya>But wait, if AI is just remixing old tasks into these new bundles, does this actually represent a fundamental shift in the macro economy? Or is this just a temporary band-aid for freelancers on Upwork trying to survive?</v>

144
00:17:14.002 --> 00:17:23.170
<v Daniel>To answer that, we have to lean on our final background paper, the 2024 Quarterly Journal of Economics paper by Autor, Chin, Solomons, and Siegmiller.</v>

145
00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:29.635
